{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n46 \n \n \n(from a launched bottle) and ISS nitrogen to the burner, allowing for on-orbit fuel dilution \nand testing at a continuum of fuel concentrations. \n \nNo samples (e.g., of soot) were collected for Flame Design; all of the ISS results were \nelectronically transmitted to the ground.  That is furthermore the case for all ACME \nexperiments. \n \nA summary log of the flames ignited during Increments 59/60 and 66, including a limited \noverview of the test conditions, is found in Table 1.  The table includes a limited overview \nof the test conditions.  For completeness, a row was included for testing on June 10 (GMT \n161) even though it was quickly discovered that the hot-wire igniter tip had failed at the \nend of preceding test day.  As a result, flames were not ignited until ten days later after \nastronaut Christina Koch had replaced the igniter tip.  \n \nKoch also swapped the porous spherical burner (serial number 1002) along with the \nigniter tip on June 10 (GMT 161), where it was determined that the initial burner\u2019s \nperformance was not satisfactory with high burner flow rates.  The flame ceased being \nspherical and its center didn\u2019t coincide with the burner center.  The results indicated that \nthe sintered attachment of the sphere to the supply tube was more porous than the \nsintered sphere itself, where the flame was shifted toward the supply tube.  The alternate \nburner (serial number 1003) showed similar behavior, but to a significantly reduced \ndegree.  As a result, some of the early Flame Design testing was repeated after the burner \nswap. \n \nPre-flight and post-flight activities are generally not addressed in this report.", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 46}}